    "narrative": "The outlook for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is anchored by **Corbin Carroll**, who stands as our clear priority at an 87.14 efficiency score. Facing Mikolas, **Carroll** benefits from a porous SP_xERA and a favorable park factor, but his true value lies in the \"Slow SP Delivery\" metric\u2014his 87% stolen base success expectation against this specific battery makes him our primary engine for the scarcest category in this 5x5 format. Similarly, **Gabriel Moreno** and **Isaac Paredes** are locked into prime spots, both boasting scores above 70 against sub-5.00 SP_xERA arms. **Austin Riley** is a fascinating case today; while his score of 71.27 is buoyed by a +16% park adjustment, his potential to capitalize on a slow delivery in the batter's box makes him a rare high-upside play despite hitting in the bottom third of the order.\n\nNavigating the lineup forced a difficult decision regarding **Seiya Suzuki**. He ultimately fell to the bench with a 47.00 score, narrowly missing the cut against Ashcraft. While **Suzuki** draws a solid lineup spot at #2, the moderate SP_xERA of 3.33 neutralized his platoon advantage, making him a riskier start than **Mickey Moniak**, who\u2014despite facing a tougher pitcher in Misiorowski\u2014projected for higher efficiency due to a significantly more favorable dynamic platoon profile. Our main point of caution remains **Josh Naylor**, who is staring down the elite arm of deGrom. While his 49.91 score keeps him in the utility slot, the prospect of deGrom\u2019s command poses a material risk to our daily AVG floor, requiring us to monitor his early at-bats closely. **Jose Caballero** also remains a marginal play at 48.26; while he is vital for speed, his efficiency is hampered by a tough matchup against the lefty S\u00e1nchez and a catcher with a 1.86s pop time, which limits his base-stealing utility today.",
